model {
  extend basalt.deployment {
    metadata {
      constraint "Before any Worker activation or container rollout, every release asserts candidate-derived hosted, Cloud, SDK, registry, model-history and Home schema readiness through bounded database-enforced read-only snapshots. Marker holes, unknown or newer versions, missing journals and unreadable state refuse with exact namespace and required/applied versions. Unreadable applied and pending values remain unknown. Candidate Home readiness also requires its native online index registry, including unchanged-image releases. Admission source pairs use the runtime adapter's publication identity carried by the barrier, never a separate Docker hash algorithm. Only a reviewed expand-compatible source pair admits one native migration invocation from the exact immutable candidate image; full readiness skips it. Image identity verifies the root manifest or index and its config independently across classic and containerd Docker stores. An independent encrypted State output records schema observations, exact image identity and the owned Docker container before starting a write. Lost replies and failed cleanup survive forward and rollback journals and refuse repeat migration until identical-image read-only proof settles the intent. A validated native failure after confirmed process exit and cleanup retains its last failure separately and permits a fresh reviewed completion only after observable schema/index or immutable image/source conditions change, or through the existing main-only manual release dispatch naming that exact failed intent. Candidate SHA or time alone is not a changed condition. A new failure consumes that acknowledgement through its new intent identity; an acknowledgement never bypasses uncertain intent or six-schema/image admission. It never loops or retries itself. Historical data-maintenance journals remain outside schema readiness."
    }
  }
}
